Yes — now I understand the exact problem much better.

You **don't just want a task checklist**. You want LoopGoal to first build a **complete repository inventory**, then map the user's goal against that inventory, and continuously verify that the AI has not missed an affected file.

For a backend with ~100 API files, for example:

```text
Goal:
"Add pagination to all user listing APIs"

             ↓

Repository Inventory
             ↓
100 API files discovered
             ↓
Relevant files identified
             ↓
Task Map
             ↓
api/v1/users/List.mo       → task-001
api/v1/admin/Users.mo      → task-002
api/v1/team/Members.mo     → task-003
...
             ↓
AI implements
             ↓
RE-SCAN repository
             ↓
Compare actual state vs Task Map
             ↓
Missing file?
   YES ─────────────→ add task
   NO
             ↓
Run skill/rule compliance
             ↓
Run verification
             ↓
Everything PASS?
   NO → continue
   YES → final audit → commit
```

That is **much more accurate** than simply asking an agent to "implement the goal."

Also, your existing project-style skills can contain very concrete architecture rules—for example, one of your stored skill files explicitly maps `Controller → Service → Repository → Storage` and says controllers must not contain business logic.  Another project document maintains an explicit file reference mapping implementation, storage, migration, and test files.  **LoopGoal should turn this kind of information into a machine-checkable execution map instead of merely putting it into the agent's prompt.**

## The feature I would add: `Repository Inventory + Task Graph`

Not just `TaskMap`.

I would make the architecture:

```text
Repository
    │
    ▼
Inventory
    │
    ├── every file
    ├── directories
    ├── modules
    ├── symbols
    ├── dependencies
    ├── project commands
    ├── skills
    ├── rules
    └── docs
    │
    ▼
Goal Analysis
    │
    ▼
Task Graph
    │
    ├── requirements
    ├── affected files
    ├── related files
    ├── dependencies
    ├── rules
    └── verification
    │
    ▼
Agent
    │
    ▼
Reconciliation
    │
    ▼
Actual Repository
```

### The important distinction

The AI should **not decide the file universe only from its context window**.

Instead LoopGoal should give it an authoritative inventory.

For example, before execution:

```bash
find . -type f \
  -not -path './.git/*' \
  -not -path './node_modules/*' \
  -not -path './vendor/*'
```

But don't stop at `find`.

Build a normalized inventory:

```text
RepositoryInventory
├── files
├── directories
├── modules
├── manifests
├── source files
├── tests
├── docs
├── configuration
├── instruction files
└── generated/ignored files
```

For a 100-file API backend:

```text
inventory.files = 247
inventory.source = 143
inventory.tests = 71
inventory.docs = 18
inventory.config = 15
```

Then the agent can reason from **the complete known repository**, not from whatever files it happened to inspect first.

